    "title": "pandas.DatetimeIndex.time — pandas 3.0.5 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.DatetimeIndex.time#\n\n- property DatetimeIndex.time[source]#\n- Returns numpy array of `datetime.time` objects.The time part of the Timestamps. See also \n  - `DatetimeIndex.timetz`\n  - Returns numpy array of `datetime.time` objects with timezones. The time part of the Timestamps.\n  - `DatetimeIndex.date`\n  - Returns numpy array of python `datetime.date` objects. Namely, the date part of Timestamps without time and timezone information.\n Examples For Series: >>> s = pd.Series([\"1/1/2020 10:00:00+00:00\", \"2/1/2020 11:00:00+00:00\"]) >>> s = pd.to_datetime(s) >>> s 0 2020-01-01 10:00:00+00:00 1 2020-02-01 11:00:00+00:00 dtype: datetime64[us, UTC] >>> s.dt.time 0 10:00:00 1 11:00:00 dtype: object For DatetimeIndex: >>> idx = pd.DatetimeIndex( ... [\"1/1/2020 10:00:00+00:00\", \"2/1/2020 11:00:00+00:00\"] ... ) >>> idx.time array([datetime.time(10, 0), datetime.time(11, 0)], dtype=object)",
    "markdown_length": 898,
